  "subtitle": {
    "en": "The four gulls you will meet almost everywhere"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "\"Seagull\" is not a bird. Britain has a dozen or more regular species of gull, and many of the ones you see in a car park are far from the sea. Today we learn the four that cover most of what you will meet: two big ones and two smaller ones.\n\nGulls are hard because they change plumage as they grow up: a young Herring Gull is brown and speckled, and takes four years to turn white and grey. To keep things easy we only look at adults today. Whenever you see a brown gull, just say \"young\" and move on.\n\nAt the end there's a quiz of eight questions, with birds from the last two weeks mixed in."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "hood"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A dark head, like a hood over a white body."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "ear spot"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A dark spot behind the eye, where the ear is."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "wing tip"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The end of the wing. On a resting gull it sticks out past the tail."
      }
    }
  ],
